29	# Disconnected environment guidance for KServe raw deployment mode
30	
31	## Description
32	
33	Author and publish a disconnected-install guide for KServe raw deployment mode in
34	the product documentation. The capability already exists and works in the product;
35	this epic closes the guidance gap by turning the steps field teams currently keep
36	in private gists into reviewed, published documentation. The guide covers
37	prerequisites, operator configuration (including which operators are not needed in
38	raw deployment mode), a complete image-mirroring reference, and post-install
39	validation, plus a troubleshooting section for the most common field-reported
40	failure modes.
41	
42	## Scope
43	
44	Content outline for the new documentation topic:
45	
46	1. **Prerequisites** — cluster and environment requirements for a disconnected
47	   (air-gapped) install of KServe in raw deployment mode; explicitly notes that
48	   Service Mesh and Serverless operators are not required in this mode.
49	2. **Operator configuration** — how to configure the KServe stack for raw
50	   deployment mode in a disconnected cluster, including which operators to skip.
51	3. **Image-mirroring reference** — the complete list of required images to mirror,
52	   and the procedure for deriving the digest list for a given release.
53	4. **Validation steps** — how to verify a disconnected install (verifying an
54	   InferenceService reaches Ready with predictor ready in raw deployment mode,
55	   without mesh dependencies).
56	5. **Troubleshooting** — the three most common field-reported failure modes:
57	   image pull errors, missing runtime, and storage-initializer misconfiguration.
58	
59	Source material exists in field enablement docs; this epic is authoring and
60	technical review work — no product behavior changes.
61	
62	## Acceptance Criteria
63	
64	- **Given** a disconnected cluster, **when** a user follows the published guide,
65	  **then** they can install and run KServe in raw deployment mode, **measured by**
66	  the guide covering prerequisites, operator configuration, and validation steps
67	  end-to-end (HLR-1).
68	- **Given** a target product release, **when** a user prepares mirror registries,
69	  **then** the guide's image-mirroring reference lists all required images and
70	  documents how to derive the digest list per release, **measured by** a reviewer
71	  confirming the mirrored image set is complete for a current release (HLR-2).
72	- **Given** the three most common field-reported failure modes (image pull errors,
73	  missing runtime, storage-initializer misconfiguration), **when** a user hits one
74	  of them, **then** the troubleshooting section covers diagnosis and remediation
75	  for each, **measured by** each failure mode having its own documented
76	  symptom/cause/remediation entry (HLR-3).
77	- **Accuracy validation (mandatory):** every technical claim in the guide is
78	  validated against the architecture context for KServe (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`)
79	  and against the actual product behavior — in particular that raw deployment mode
80	  uses plain Deployments/Services with no Service Mesh / Serverless dependency,
81	  and that no operator or default-configuration change is implied. Any
82	  discrepancy found in the docs source material is corrected, not transcribed.
83	- **Technical review:** the guide is technically reviewed against the
84	  implementation by the KServe owning team before publication (Red Hat docs
85	  content process).
86	- No code, operator, or default-configuration changes are introduced by this work
87	  (strategy out-of-scope constraint is respected).
88	
89	## HLR Traceability
90	
91	- **HLR-1** (P1): Disconnected install guide for raw deployment mode —
92	  prerequisites, operator configuration, validation steps → this epic (scope
93	  items 1, 2, 4).
94	- **HLR-2** (P1): Complete image-mirroring reference → this epic (scope item 3).
95	- **HLR-3** (P2): Troubleshooting section for three common failure modes → this
96	  epic (scope item 5).
97	
98	All three HLRs are authoring work on the same doc topic by the same team, sharing
99	the same content surface; they are bundled in this single epic rather than split —
100	the P2 troubleshooting section is not independently shippable user value separate
101	from the guide it lives in.
